1
00:00:02,570 --> 00:00:04,830
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:06,040
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:09,410
My name is Catherine and today we have another holiday lesson for you guys.

4
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:14,390
It's an intermediate level lesson, but it's not about Christmas and it's not about New Year's.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:16,000
So what's this holiday, Marco?

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:17,990
It's like after Christmas, but before New Year's.

7
00:00:18,210 --> 00:00:21,690
Okay, so let's look at this holiday in today's vocabulary preview.

8
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:24,000
Vocabulary preview.

9
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:28,880
All right, so as you noticed in the title, this is called Boxing Day.

10
00:00:29,590 --> 00:00:32,320
So like fighting boxing or...

11
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:33,320
No, no, no.

12
00:00:33,320 --> 00:00:34,400
Kind of like boxes, right?

13
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:35,610
It's more related to boxes.

14
00:00:36,010 --> 00:00:36,150
Exactly.

15
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:40,840
So this is a holiday that we don't actually celebrate in America.

16
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:43,350
It's celebrated in what are called Commonwealth countries.

17
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:47,910
That means countries that used to be a part of Great Britain or that are a part of Great Britain.

18
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:51,270
So England, Scotland, Canada, Australia.

19
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:56,570
And this is a day where, well, it's considered a holiday because it's a bank holiday.

20
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:58,460
That means all the banks close.

21
00:00:58,460 --> 00:00:58,990
No one's at work.

22
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:00,610
It's the day after Christmas.

23
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:06,790
So it's December 26th and it's traditionally a day when you give money or gifts to the poor.

24
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:08,600
Okay.

25
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:13,070
And so this is more or less what we're going to be talking about, but more modern, right?

26
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:16,670
Before it was like that, but now it's kind of become this shopping thing, right?

27
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:17,450
It has.

28
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:22,470
It's a day when everyone goes to the sales after Christmas to return things or to buy new things.

29
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:26,270
So we're going to hear about this right away in today's dialogue.

30
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:39,360
And when we come back, we'll be talking about some of the phrases and words that you've heard.

31
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:40,060
What do you think of this one?

32
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:41,360
Eh, so-so.

33
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:42,360
And this one?

34
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:43,320
Too flashy?

35
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:44,360
Nah, not too flashy.

36
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:47,450
Oh, and this sweater from my aunt.

37
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:48,310
Isn't it hideous?

38
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:49,310
I guess.

39
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:50,570
Are you even listening?

40
00:01:50,930 --> 00:01:52,350
I'm trying to have a conversation with you.

41
00:01:52,730 --> 00:01:56,290
And I'm trying to watch the game, but you're yapping on about your new clothes.

42
00:01:56,950 --> 00:02:02,470
Well I have to decide which gifts to keep and which to exchange for better ones when I go to the Boxing Day sales this afternoon.

43
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:05,590
Well could you do me the favor of making this quick?

44
00:02:05,930 --> 00:02:08,530
It's the third quarter and you've been blabbering on since the first.

45
00:02:09,090 --> 00:02:10,270
Oh, your precious game.

46
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:16,860
You watch the same game every year and each year your beloved hometown team loses by at least three goals.

47
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:18,840
Oh, no you didn't.

48
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,820
You didn't just insult the Salisbury Seals, did you?

49
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:27,640
Why don't you just go return all those stupid clothes and not come back until the sales are over?

50
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:28,720
I might just.

51
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,220
Enjoy your stupid game.

52
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:31,560
And Merry Christmas.

53
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:32,380
Merry Christmas.

54
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:44,190
Alright, great, so we're back and interesting, the couple fighting right after Christmas.

55
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:48,250
Well I think it's very common to hear fights around the holidays.

56
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:50,840
People are very stressed out.

57
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:56,000
And tired of dealing with family and presents and all the stress, right?

58
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Exactly.

59
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:58,570
But we'll talk about that a little bit later on.

60
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:03,070
Right now why don't we take a look at four key words in language takeaway.

61
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:06,160
Language takeaway.

62
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:14,680
Okay, these first two words are words that the wife uses in talking about some of these clothes that she got as presents.

63
00:03:15,620 --> 00:03:19,320
So she says, well she asks her husband, is this too flashy?

64
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:21,560
Flashy.

65
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:24,960
Alright, so you know flash, like the camera flash, right?

66
00:03:24,960 --> 00:03:26,280
This big light.

67
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:32,840
Now flashy when you're talking about clothes or an object, it's too noticeable, right?

68
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:35,380
It's too noticeable, too bright, too loud.

69
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:38,680
So for example, a black shirt is not flashy.

70
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:40,660
But an orange shirt.

71
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:48,530
An orange shirt with buttons and bells and rhinestones, diamonds, that's flashy.

72
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:54,240
She can also mean something that is supposed to look expensive.

73
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:58,790
Okay, so that sweater was a little bit flashy according to her.

74
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:03,770
But also she talked about another sweater being hideous.

75
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:07,870
So hideous is an ugly word and it means ugly.

76
00:04:08,450 --> 00:04:08,690
Ugly.

77
00:04:09,170 --> 00:04:11,430
Okay, so I could say this sweater is hideous.

78
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:15,160
That means it's not only ugly, it's really ugly.

79
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:16,580
Okay, it's more than ugly.

80
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:18,400
Alright, a hideous sweater.

81
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:27,760
And moving on, the guy now was a little bit irritated and stressed out and he said, you keep yapping on about your new clothes.

82
00:04:28,280 --> 00:04:30,180
So what is this yapping on?

83
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:34,760
Well, yap is a sound like yap, yap, yap, yap, yap, yap, yap, yap.

84
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:41,600
So yapping, to yap on about means to continue to talk and talk and talk about something.

85
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:48,600
Because he's watching the football game on TV and she's talking and talking and talking and he's annoyed.

86
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:53,690
So this is rude to say, stop yapping about this or stop yapping on about your game.

87
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:55,030
It's very rude.

88
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:56,680
It means just stop talking.

89
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:56,970
I'm annoyed.

90
00:04:57,710 --> 00:05:01,670
And well, then he mentioned another phrase that is pretty much the same thing, right?

91
00:05:02,350 --> 00:05:03,170
Blabbering on.

92
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:10,210
To blabber, to blabber on about means to talk about something but to not really make sense.

93
00:05:10,670 --> 00:05:13,270
Okay, so it's just worthless talk.

94
00:05:13,650 --> 00:05:15,390
So you're just talking to talk.

95
00:05:15,690 --> 00:05:17,770
You're not really saying anything interesting.

96
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:18,510
Exactly.

97
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:26,160
And so sometimes we make fun of people who blabber on about things because they're not important to us or they sound stupid.

98
00:05:26,160 --> 00:05:27,160
All right.

99
00:05:27,250 --> 00:05:29,410
Okay, so four key words there.

100
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:31,280
Blabbering on, yapping on.

101
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:33,820
Why don't we listen to them again in the dialogue?

102
00:05:39,160 --> 00:05:40,260
What do you think of this one?

103
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:41,840
Eh, so-so.

104
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:42,780
And this one?

105
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:43,500
Too flashy?

106
00:05:44,290 --> 00:05:45,210
Nah, not too flashy.

107
00:05:45,830 --> 00:05:48,490
Oh, and this sweater from my aunt, isn't it hideous?

108
00:05:49,050 --> 00:05:49,430
I guess.

109
00:05:49,990 --> 00:05:50,750
Are you even listening?

110
