Well, could you do me the favor of making this quick?

114
00:06:06,090 --> 00:06:08,710
It's the third quarter and you've been blabbering on since the first.

115
00:06:09,180 --> 00:06:10,400
Oh, your precious game.

116
00:06:11,180 --> 00:06:17,060
You watch the same game every year and each year your beloved hometown team loses by at least three goals.

117
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:19,040
Oh, no you didn't.

118
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:21,980
You didn't just insult the Salisbury Seals, did you?

119
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:27,940
Why don't you just go return all those stupid clothes and not come back until the sales are over?

120
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:28,920
I might just.

121
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:30,440
Enjoy your stupid game.

122
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:31,680
And Merry Christmas.

123
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:32,580
Merry Christmas.

124
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:37,720
All right, we're back.

125
00:06:37,720 --> 00:06:41,390
So now we have a couple of different phrases that we want to look at on Fluency Builder.

126
00:06:43,800 --> 00:06:44,150
Fluency Builder.

127
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:51,980
Okay, so this first phrase is very closely related to the days after Christmas, especially here on Boxing Day.

128
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:59,220
We've got someone who's talking about going to the stores, to the sales to exchange her gifts for better gifts.

129
00:07:00,060 --> 00:07:02,260
Okay, to exchange something for something better.

130
00:07:02,940 --> 00:07:04,560
This is a very common construction.

131
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:06,440
It's an important phrase when talking about shopping.

132
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:13,840
So when we look at this construction, you would say, to exchange this sweater for a better one or for a larger one.

133
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:14,070
Exactly.

134
00:07:14,840 --> 00:07:17,510
So you can change the adjective, the word that's describing it.

135
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:19,970
So you can say, to exchange these shoes for bigger ones.

136
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:26,720
I could also joke and I could say, I'm going to exchange my boyfriend for a better one if he's being rude to me.

137
00:07:26,720 --> 00:07:29,770
Okay, so to exchange something for something else.

138
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:31,280
Exactly.

139
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:32,280
All right.

140
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:37,010
And then we saw another phrase that the husband replied to when she offended him with his football team.

141
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:39,250
He said, oh no, you didn't.

142
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:40,100
Didn't what?

143
00:07:40,920 --> 00:07:41,320
I know, didn't what?

144
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:42,180
No, you didn't what?

145
00:07:42,920 --> 00:07:47,420
All right, so this, like you said, doesn't make sense alone, but some cultural context might help.

146
00:07:48,080 --> 00:07:54,380
In English, we often say this phrase to mean, oh no, you didn't just say that.

147
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:56,240
Oh, you didn't just insult my team.

148
00:07:56,540 --> 00:07:59,920
He's saying, I can't believe you just said that.

149
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:04,100
He's upset because what she said was rude or mean.

150
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:05,560
Okay.

151
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:08,060
So I could say, you know, Marco, I think Ecuador sucks.

152
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:10,200
Oh, no, you didn't.

153
00:08:10,400 --> 00:08:15,100
I did just say, I don't think so actually, but it's usually a response, right?

154
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:25,000
You have to wait until somebody maybe says something mean or something that, you know, really hurts her feelings or you don't agree with when you respond to this, but you're usually very, very offended, right?

155
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:26,400
Exactly.

156
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:30,040
It's always a response to a comment that has made you angry or upset.

157
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:31,040
Okay.

158
00:08:31,040 --> 00:08:38,280
And then our last phrase is when they finished talking and they were fighting, she left and said, I might just.

159
00:08:39,320 --> 00:08:39,500
Just what?

160
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:40,580
I know, just what?

161
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:44,420
See, again, these are common phrases, but they don't seem to make much sense alone.

162
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:46,260
I might just.

163
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:48,640
So let's listen to, this is a response again.

164
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:50,900
This is a response to the line before it.

165
00:08:51,980 --> 00:08:59,800
Why don't you just, just go and return all of those stupid clothes and not come back until much later?

166
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:06,340
And she says, I might just, I might just do exactly what you just said.

167
00:09:06,760 --> 00:09:06,880
Okay.

168
00:09:07,760 --> 00:09:07,900
She's threatening him.

169
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:11,060
She's saying, I might just go out and not come back for 10 hours.

170
00:09:11,340 --> 00:09:11,600
All right.

171
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:12,620
I might just do that.

172
00:09:13,060 --> 00:09:13,340
Okay.

173
00:09:13,580 --> 00:09:15,300
So that's a way to say what you said.

174
00:09:15,560 --> 00:09:16,340
I might do that.

175
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:17,860
And I'm threatening you.

176
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:20,160
So again, it's usually a response, right?

177
00:09:20,920 --> 00:09:22,400
Or a threat, as you said.

178
00:09:23,240 --> 00:09:25,340
So I can say, Marco, why don't you just leave?

179
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:26,100
All right.

180
00:09:26,260 --> 00:09:26,780
I might just.

181
00:09:27,600 --> 00:09:30,840
Oh, what am I going to do in the studio alone?

182
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:31,840
All right.

183
